lly Republican, leaving this a genuine coin-flip with the faintest Democratic edge.", "watch": "Fresh independent polling, a late surge of outside spending, or a shift in the national environment over the final weeks could easily tip this either way.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Bill Huizenga (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Michigan's 4th District in the southwest corner of the state is one of the closest House races on the 2026 map. Republican Representative Bill Huizenga seeks another term against Democrat Sean McCann, with a Green Party candidate also on the ballot. The district leaned Republican by three points at the last presidential election, but the national environment this year appears to favor Democrats, largely canceling that structural edge.\n\nHuizenga holds the financial advantage, having raised about $4.1 million to McCann's $2.6 million, though outside groups have spent more than $1.1 million attacking him while McCann has drawn scant outside support. Expert ratings split: three call the race Lean Republican, five call it a Tossup, and one calls it Lean Democratic. The only September poll, taken for a Democratic sponsor, showed McCann ahead 47-46, and prediction markets give Republicans a slight edge. Turnout and the national tide will likely decide it.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.5309, "margin": 0.42}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.4667, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.4711, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.4732, "margin": -1.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.4842, "margin": 0.3}]}